 "drivingQuestion": "Does the mishna's bar on presumption between father and son hold after they separated?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "Decided in the following unit.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_batra.52a.6",
   "daf": "52a",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "וְלֹא לָאָב בְּנִכְסֵי הַבֵּן, וְלֹא לַבֵּן בְּנִכְסֵי הָאָב. אָמַר רַב יוֹסֵף: אֲפִילּוּ חָלְקוּ. רָבָא אָמַר: חָלְקוּ – לֹא.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And a father does not have» the ability to establish the presumption of ownership «with regard to a son’s property, and a son does not have» the ability to establish the presumption of ownership «with regard to a father’s property. Rav Yosef says: Even» if «they separated» and the son is no longer dependent on his father, the presumption of ownership still cannot be established by a father or son with regard to the other’s property. «Rava says:» If «they separated,» that is «not» the halakha, and the presumption of ownership can be established."
  }
 ],
